<section class="sleep-chapter" id="prologue">
<p>When I first saw Kisako Yama, I thought I finally was able to fulfill a lifelong dream.</p>
<p>Since I was a child, I already decided that I wanted to be with someone. As childish as that thought was, it started to blossom, and bloomed, into a full-blown need to have a partner.</p>
<p>Or at least, paired with someone that I truly love from the first sight, and they also love me at first sight.</p>
<p>The lady in front of me is elegant, beautiful. Straight dark hair, perfectly manicured fingers. Pursed lips, thin figure wrapped in the customary white gown worn by brides, while I stood awkwardly wearing the customary dark suit for a groom.</p>
<p>But that euphoria soon faded, and replace with grim reality, as I saw the glimpse of her looking at me, and I can see it as clear as the sky during daytime. She wasn't fond of me. I was sporting a messy black hair myself, looking pathetic with black bag under my eyes that the make up artist desperately trying to cover, and standing almost hunchbacked.</p>
<p>I sighed inwardly. Of course, it was just a childhood dream. An errant wish that was born out of a child's desire, as useless as a shooting star that nobody witnessed.</p>
<p>"Kisako Yama, do you take... Grant Illa, as your lawfully wedded husband?"</p>
<p>She shifted her eyes towards the ground, making sure not to look around and seeing the many eyes that are observing the ceremony. Or the guards with their weapons.</p>
<p>"I... do."</p>
<p>The arbitrator, as we call those who wed people now, smiled with satisfaction after waiting with bated breath when Kisako didn't reply, and turned towards me.</p>
<p>"And you, Grant Illa, do you take Kisako Yama as your lawfully wedded wife?"</p>
<p>I did not shift my eyes as I look Kisako straight in the eyes, and answered as resolutely as I can.</p>
<p>"I do."</p>
<p>"So by the power bestowed to me by the United..."</p>
<p>The words began to blur after that as we were both ushered away, to take off our gown and suits, then to where? I don't even know.</p>
<p>But, it was official. I am now married... or at least, "paired", to someone else. Someone that I didn't even pick.</p>
<p>Someone that the government arranged for me to marry.</p>
<p class="sleep-reveal">COVER</p>
</section>

<section class="sleep-chapter" id="chap1">
<h2 class="sleep-chapter__title">Kisako <span>I</span></h2>
<p>I am fucked.</p>
<p>I am so, truly fucked.</p>
<hr class="sleep-break">
<p>That's the sentence that kept bouncing on my mind as I sit there with my new "husband", Mr. Illa, and listening to the new couple's counsel speaking on our newly married arrangement of living in a room that look akin to a standard psychologist's room with couch and all.</p>
<p>When I heard that I was picked to be paired with someone, I feel trepidation. I know it was inevitable. I was almost 30 years old yet remain unmarried, not even in relationship.</p>
<p>At least, that's what I report to the watcher. This almost invisible, permeable device that I have within me at all times as it attach itself to my belongings. There are comparisons to be made to a certain book about my current living condition, one can say it starts with the word "Big" and ends with the word "Brother".</p>
<p>It look different for everyone, and as much as the united government would like to claim that it doesn't record audio of our life, I would never be able to live without it now. It was required for payment, identification, and basically functioning for day-to-day life. It serves as a replacement for my phone, my ID, and also... my social media.</p>
<p>Gone the days where social media are separate and competing, now it's all melding into a singular network that almost works like all of them combined. I can post status and look for networking for a job, or watch videos in the Videos section.</p>
<p>Or, as I say, "report" about my relationship status.</p>
<hr class="sleep-break">
<p>"...Ms. Illa?"</p>
<p>I did not notice that the counsel is calling for me, in a name I did not realize. Then it hit me. I sheepishly smiled as I pay attention to them.</p>
<p>"I'm sorry... that's something I have to get used to now, isn't it?"</p>
<p>The counsel laughed it off and say, "You are not the first one to space out during this session, miss, you don't need to worry."</p>
<p>My new husband, Mr. Illa, laughed as well, albeit a bit more awkwardly. I noticed that he looked to my arms as if he's thinking of doing something, perhaps to comfort me. I almost rolled my eyes before I grabbed his hand.</p>
<p>I have to play my part now.</p>
<p>The counsel noticed the... sweet... gesture and continued with a small awkward cough, "Anyway... your belongings have been deposited to the new place, along with, as you know, a new watcher designed for those that we paired. I hope my explanation regarding the mandatory audit and features on the new place have been satisfactory."</p>
<p>I feel Mr. Illa's hands tightly grip me when the counsel mentioned the "mandatory audit", but he stopped soon afterwards. What was that about?</p>
<p>"It has been, Mr. Joel." My husband said with a smile too wide to be called normal.</p>
<hr class="sleep-break">
<p>We both stood up and bowed, as Mr. Joel the counsel do the same, and I followed my husband as he did not let go of my hand.</p>
<p>"Mr. Illa." I said slowly as we walked down the hall outside the counsel room, towards our accomodation to reach the new place. "Can you let go of my hand? I can walk by myself."</p>
<p>He turned around to look at me and blushed slightly as he let go of my hand. "Ah, I apologize... do you prefer if I don't..."</p>
<p>I nodded to him as I walk forward, whispering softly. "We don't have to be affable when we're alone, Mr. Illa."</p>
<p>I didn't see his expression, but I can feel him hurriedly followed behind me. "I... see. But at the very least... you can call me Grant."</p>
<p>"I will do no such thing." I shook my head fervently, not even looking back.</p>
<p>This is a marriage of convenience. I'll play my part, but only when it was appropriate.</p>
<p>"If we are going to make this work..." He said, and I can hear his exasperated tone. "...at the very least, we should get used to calling each other our first name."</p>
<p>I stopped as he said that and turned to look to him, and seeing his desperate face, a memory seems to overlap my vision at that moment. A familiar face, overlaying Mr. Illa's, and I caught the gasp that was about to come out of my throat, before I cover it up with a small cough.</p>
<p>I looked away as well. He's in a better place now, hopefully with someone better. I should stop thinking about him.</p>
<p>"You're right. I guess... you can call me Kisako."</p>
<p>I eyed him from the side, and noticed his eyes lightening up slightly. "Nice to meet you, Kisako." He then offers his hand with a small smile. "I will be in your care."</p>
<p>I look at his extended arm and then back to him again, before sighing and meets it with my own hand. "Perhaps... I will be in your care too, Grant."</p>
</section>

<section class="sleep-chapter" id="chap2">
<h2 class="sleep-chapter__title">Grant <span>II</span></h2>
<p>She doesn't seem to hate me that much.</p>
<p>That's the only thought I can gather after being together with Kisako for a few days. For as sudden as our pairing was, according to the program of the united government, it seems like their algorithm managed to pair a like-minded individual.</p>
<p>It pains me to admit that, since this is not exactly what I wanted for my life as well, but it was inevitable. I was unable to properly become a member of society, with no partner to report to the watcher, as I reached the age of thirty-one.</p>
<p>Such is what I reported, not entirely the truth, however.</p>
<hr class="sleep-break">
<p>The morning of the third day comes, and I woke up, with Kisako beside me. She is wearing a white pajamas given by the government, that I also wore, which should be replaced as soon as we enter the workforce available to couples in the coming days. Her long black hair seems to invade portion of my bed that we partitioned off, decided during our first night together.</p>
<hr class="sleep-break">
<p>"Do we really have to do this?" I remember saying that to Kisako, who stared at me like I just disrespected her.</p>
<p>"Yes. You should consider yourself lucky that I still am willing to let you sleep on the same bed with me." She then said with a low voice. "This is still an act."</p>
<p>I sighed. "Yes, I understand that. I just thought this to be a bit... excessive, that's all." I looked at the partition, extra pillows taken from under the bed, which makes it look more like a "pillow fort's wall" than anything.</p>
</section>

